        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="0" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

There was air leakage observed around the frames of windows and doors, however the windows and doors appeared to be ok. I recommend pulling back the trim and sealing around the frame. Foam rope is an excellent tool for these areas, or low expansion spray foam can be used as well if the gap is fairly large. As you mentioned replacing the windows and doors, ensure the new installs are well sealed with the wall. 

The majority of the air leakage is coming from the crawlspace, I was able to identify several 'weak' spots along the header space (where the concrete meets the wood floor members). 

All of the attic access hatchs had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatchs. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 50 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.6 GJ.

I suspect a 20% or better improvement on the air leakage is quite achievable by tackling the air leakage in the crawlspace. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1x4 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior main walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house appeared to have 10.0 inches (R32) of fiberglass insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, specifically spray foam, I have modelled adding an additional 2 inches of spray foam (R10)  insulation for a total insulation value of R42.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R42, you could potentially save $ 60 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.9 GJ. 



Although the savings are fairly minimal, the air sealing benefits of spray foam could be quite significant, as air leakage is one of the larger heat loss areas for your home. If you decide to add any additional insulation into the attic space, please ensure to check that the ventilation is not restricted with the additional insulation. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of (R-50). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x10 joists with R28 fiberglass batt insulation. As you mentioned the possibility of spray foam in the crawl space, I have modelled adding an additional 2 inches of spray foam the floor above the crawl space. 



Savings:

By adding 2 inches (R10) of spray foam between the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $ 35 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.8 GJ.



The larger energy consumption is the air leakage, which a significant ammount is coming from the crawlspace. By applying sprayfoam into the header spaces, i suspect this will significantly help with the air leakage.  If you decide to apply sprayfoam in the crawlspace, I recommend to keep an eye on the crawlspace areas for moisture/humidity problems. Removing the natural air infiltration by air sealing, you may need to investigate a deticated ventilation system for the crawlspace. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane wood framed ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the  windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing all 8 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 240 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.7 GJ.  



Note: The count of 8 windows includes the those associated with the exterior doors, as you mentioned replacing these as well. I have modeled these windows as triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, picture windows. </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre propane-fired hot water tank, which has a manufacturer energy factor rating of 0.62. I recommend that you replace your hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>DOORS



Your exterior doors appear to be in reasonable shape, however the frames are poorly sealed in terms of air leakage. If you do replace these doors with new ones, I recommend replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR DUCT



I recommend you install an electric damper on the combustion air duct and tie it to the operation of your heating system and water heater. This will ensure fresh air will only be introduced into the room when the heating system or water heater is operating. By installing an electric damper on the combustion air duct, you could save energy and increase your EnerGuide rating. There are no rebates for installing a damper on the combustion air duct, however it will increase your homes airtightness. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
